## Part 2 — Automatic spell check with author-name exemptions

### Design principle

The checker has exactly one protected class of terms: **names of people and organizations that git attributes to the code being checked.** Those terms are treated as correct by definition. Everything else is fair game.

### Protected versus corrected

| Term type | Example | Behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| Contributor surname | `Okonkwo` | Protected — never flagged, never rewritten |
| Contributor handle | `@ada-lovelace` | Protected |
| Organization in `CODEOWNERS` | `@acme-platform-team` | Protected |
| `Signed-off-by` name | `Client Contractor` | Protected |
| Ordinary misspelling | `recieve` | Corrected to `receive` |
| Domain jargon | `webhook`, `idempotent` | Protected via a project dictionary, not via authorship |
| Identifier in code | `getUsrRecrd()` | Skipped by default; opt in per-language |

### Correction versus protection precedence

When a token matches both a protected name and a known misspelling — for example a surname that coincides with a common typo — **protection wins.** The extension logs the collision to the attribution panel so a maintainer can resolve it deliberately rather than letting an autocorrect silently rename a person.

