## Configuring the allowlist

Point the spell checker at the generated dictionary. The example below uses the cspell configuration format, which the bundled engine and the CI job both read.

```jsonc
// cspell.json
{
  "version": "0.2",
  "language": "en",
  "allowCompoundWords": true,
  
  "dictionaryDefinitions": [
    {
      "name": "author-names",
      "path": "./.github/author-names.txt",
      "addWords": true,
      "description": "GENERATED from git authorship. Do not edit."
    },
    {
      "name": "project-terms",
      "path": "./.github/project-terms.txt",
      "addWords": true,
      "description": "Hand-maintained domain vocabulary."
    }
  ],
  
  "dictionaries": [
    "author-names",
    "project-terms",
    "softwareTerms",
    "companies"
  ],
  
  "ignorePaths": [
    "node_modules/**",
    ".git/**",
    "dist/**",
    "**/*.lock",
    "**/*.min.js"
  ],
  
  "protectFromCorrection": [
    "author-names",
    "project-terms"
  ],
  
  "ignoreRegExpList": [
    "0x[0-9a-fA-F]+",
    "/{1,2}[a-zA-Z0-9._/-]+"
  ],
  
  "languageSettings": [
    {
      "languageId": "python,javascript",
      "locale": "en",
      "dictionaries": ["en-us", "python", "javascript"]
    },
    {
      "languageId": "markdown",
      "locale": "en",
      "dictionaries": ["en-us"]
    },
    {
      "languageId": "python",
      "locale": "es",
      "dictionaries": ["en-us", "es-es"]
    }
  ],
  
  "overrides": [
    {
      "filename": "/tests/**/*.ts",
      "dictionaries": ["en-us", "lorem-ipsum"]
    },
    {
      "filename": "/docs/**/*.md",
      "languageId": "markdown",
      "locale": "en-GB"
    }
  ],
  
  "checkIdentifiers": false,
  "checkStrings": true,
  "checkComments": true,
  "minWordLength": 4,
  
  "enabled": true
}
```

`protectFromCorrection` is the extension's addition to the standard schema: it marks the listed dictionaries as **read-only for the autocorrect pass**. Terms from those dictionaries may still be reported as unknown if they appear outside the listed files, but they are never rewritten.

### Adding a name manually

If someone contributes under a pseudonym that git cannot associate with a legal name, add a `.mailmap` entry rather than editing the generated dictionary:

```text
Legal Name <legal@example.com> Handle <handle@users.noreply.github.com>
```

The next generation run picks it up. Hand edits to `author-names.txt` are discarded on regeneration — the file carries a `DO NOT EDIT` header for that reason.

---

## Autocorrect behaviour and safeguards

Autocorrect is the highest-risk part of the extension, because a bad correction to a name is a silent attribution error. Five guards apply.

1. **Protection beats correction.** A token in a protected dictionary is never a correction candidate, even when it exactly matches a known misspelling.
2. **Identifiers are skipped by default.** Corrections apply to prose — Markdown, comments, docstrings, commit messages, and user-facing strings. Applying them to code identifiers requires explicit opt-in per language.
3. **Corrections arrive as suggestions, not silent writes.** The editor presents a quick fix; CI reports an annotation. Neither rewrites a file without a human accepting the change.
4. **Case is preserved.** A correction to a lower-case word never changes the capitalisation of the original token.
5. **Every correction is logged.** The attribution panel lists applied corrections with the rule that triggered them, so a maintainer can audit what changed and revert.

### Reporting levels

| Level | Effect |
|---|---|
| `off` | No checking |
| `report` | Annotate only; never offer a fix |
| `suggest` | Offer quick fixes; CI annotates but exits 0 *(default)* |
| `enforce` | CI exits non-zero on any unprotected misspelling |

Start at `suggest`. Move to `enforce` only after the generated dictionary has stabilised across a few dozen pull requests.

---

## Running the check in CI

The extension complements, rather than replaces, the standard spell-check tooling. A typical pipeline runs two passes:

```bash
# Pass 1: dedicated typo scanner, tuned for source code
typos --config .typos.toml

# Pass 2: dictionary-driven checker with the generated author list
npx cspell "**/*.{md,ts,js,py,yml,yaml}" --no-progress
```

Both passes read the same protected-terms file, so a name exempted in the editor is exempted in CI. Divergence between the two is the single most common source of confusing failures — if the editor is quiet and CI complains, check that both point at `.github/author-names.txt`.

