## Installing the extension

### As a VS Code extension

```bash
code --install-extension <PUBLISHER>.blame-attrib-spellcheck
```

The extension bundles a spell-check engine and reads the same dictionary files as the CI job, so editor and pipeline agree.

### As a GitHub App

Install from the repository's **Settings → GitHub Apps** page. Grant `contents: read` and `metadata: read`. No write scopes are requested; the extension never pushes commits.

### As a GitHub Action

```yaml
# .github/workflows/attribution-spellcheck.yml
name: Attribution and spell check

on:
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: [main]

permissions:
  contents: read
  pull-requests: write   # only to post the attribution report

jobs:
  check:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0   # blame needs full history

      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20

      - name: Generate protected terms from authorship
        run: python scripts/gen_author_dict.py .github/author-names.txt

      - name: Spell check
        run: npx --yes cspell "**/*.{md,ts,js,py,yml,yaml}" --no-progress

      - name: Post attribution report
        if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'
        run: npx --yes <PUBLISHER>/blame-attrib-spellcheck report --pr "${{ github.event.number }}"
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> `fetch-depth: 0` is required. A shallow clone truncates history, which truncates the allowlist, which produces false positives on contributor names.

